; An anonymous `~` is a value: `=` names it, and a call site runs it.
;
; Lambda needed no operator either — `~` may omit its name, and what it produces
; is the syntax value the language already had.
;
; Three calls: the arrow inside the lambda fires its subject and then its own
; stage, and the report fires once. The numeric lambda beside it is applied
; directly in operator position — how a Lisp writes a lambda call — and costs
; nothing at all, which is why three and not four.
; expect: calls 3
; expect: clean
; expect: contains 25
; expect: answers
(= describe (~ (subject) (-> ,subject "Name one failure mode of this, in four words."))
   (= note (describe "a retry queue that drops messages on restart")
      ($ "Reply with exactly: the square is "
         |((~ (n) |([product] ,n ,n)|) 5)|
         " and the note was: " note)))
